{"_about":"A listening test of evening out a slur's note levels by CC11 (branch even-levels-test, 2026-10-12): Whence 12-TET bars 1-4, Celli - Performance alone, as MuseScore plays it (N) and with a per-note CC11 correction moving as a step (S), a ramp over each transition (R) or a glide between notes (G). Rendered on the Windows test VM: kthost (offline VST 3 host), Kontakt 8 + SSO, Celli - Performance from MuseScore's resaved setup (library defaults), 44.1 kHz, three passes per render (round robins advancing), no patch volume (MuseScore's +6 dB slot volume is a constant gain). Levels: perceived (tools/playbackverify/loudness.py, ~dB) over 136.4 ms from note-on + the map's legatoDelay (sso_slur_swell.json's window), median of the passes; notes 1 (an attack from silence) and 64 (window after its note-off) left out of the slur statistics. correction: target (the slur's median, N) - N's level. variants.<V>.cc11ReN: the CC11 sent at each note (S, R: from its note-on; G: at its window's middle), dB re N's, solved so the curve (smoothed 35 ms as the envelope) flattens N's measured envelope to the slur medians (5 ms grid, least squares) - the heard window lags the note-on by 200-240 ms, more than the 136 ms IOI, so a note-on's value mostly corrects the note heard then; shift: dB the whole curve was lowered to stay within 127. <V>2: one more iteration from that variant's own measurement (made things no better: the measured level moved ~1.2-1.3 x the CC11 dB predicted, plus pass-to-pass noise ~0.4 dB per note). stats: per slur max - min (dB). tools/soundlibraries/even_levels_test.py has the method. Measurement only: the renderer does not read this file.","describe":{"N":"as MuseScore plays it now (fork dump, Recommended): CC11 127 then the level rule's value (102 bars 1 and 3, 109 bars 2 and 4) at every note-on","S":"step: CC11 jumps to a per-note value at each note-on (64 messages)","R":"ramp: CC11 moves linearly (in CC value) from the note before's value to this note's fr